u kind of founded  punch taverns one of the biggest pub  chains  involved in setting up of pizza express  as well  i know how hard it is to quantify these  things but you know how many businesses  could be at risk hit well obviously it  does depend on what happens next  uh we appear to have had um thanks to  perhaps  you know some good work by uh sir by  certain mps for which  uh you know we in the industry are very  grateful we appear to have had a bit of  a rowing back from  the idea of these restrictions all the  way through  till easter but if they were to go that  long  obviously depending somewhat on other  forms of government support  if they were to go on that long you  would be looking at millions of job  losses just in hospitality  let alone retail where of course we've  seen arcadia fail  and you know i i would just make the  point i know others have made it  but in in terms of um the risk  you know the average age of people we  employees in in their early 20s and most  of our customers  and that's also true of retail  and since the epidemic if that's what it  is since  it started the total number of healthy  people  under 40 who've actually sadly died of  the disease  has been just 46. you know that's 46  46 not 46 million 46 people out of 55  million  under 40 who were healthy have died of  this and we need to get it in proportion  because he is destroying  the lives of many people who are in  their 20s  when they have absolutely zero risk  of this disease you know my son's friend  from university was saying  yesterday that at their university 1300  students have  tested positive not a single one has  even been hospital  uh i don't even know if any of them been  see the doctor so you're destroying the  lives of a generation that actually  doesn't suffer from the disease and they  need to balance  that terrible destruction with the  possible good they are saying and they  need to think of other measures that  might save more lives  and and create less destruction you  could be seeing you know a million or  two million  unemployed just from retail and  hospitality  um depending on what happens next  what the government would argue is that  the best way to  help the economy is to suppress the  virus because it means that if we  get a grip on it then we can get back to  normal  more quickly and they would argue that  yes although of course age is a big  factor in how ill people get  they're also worried about young  people's ability to spread the virus as  well  i mean they do need to balance their  health risks as well don't they  well you know they produce there's a  very large study as you probably know so  if you know by the nhs which is 12 and a  half million  adults and there is actually absolutely  no  evidence uh that even in families where  there are members over 65  the the children being at school and  what have you increased the risk to  those adults in fact there's  some evidence that somehow perhaps by  spread  by the immunity from um other viruses  which we now know exists you know such  as uh common colds not immunity but  resistance  that in fact parents of children who go  to school  and perhaps have had these costs of  colds have a slightly lower risk  and this is in a study of 12 and a half  million adults  so i think this whole idea that you can  suppress it in  society and that makes people safer you  know a long study in the lancet  also one in the british medical journal  the two most reputable journals  in the uk both say that lockdowns and  measures do not save lives  they may possibly postpone the event in  which case perhaps the var  the vaccine is helpful but the reality  is it would have always been  far easier to protect the elderly  i mean the only thing probably we knew  in january about this disease from  the outbreak in china you know if we  knew just one single thing  we knew that it was very very dangerous  for the very old and the very ill now if  i'd known that  the single thing i would have done would  have been to protect care homes and  people in geriatric wards  and yet as we heard from a member of  sage the other day they actually didn't  even think about it  it is not a measure in the imperial  model in the imperial model that models  for the different  non-pharmaceutical inventions as they  politely call  idiotic measures like lockdowns there is  not even an intervention that involves  protecting care homes  and you heard this chat this professor  last week saying well we just didn't  think about  it we didn't ask whether they were being  protected we assumed it  that could have saved half the lives  that had been lost  in that one measure instead they locked  down the entire country  for no reason okay i'm pretty clear that  you've got some strong views on the  impact  of lockdown and you've previously  donated to the conservative party would  you say that the conservatives are still  friends of business and  not at the moment look i i think the the  treasury  is responding in whatever way it can  and actually doing a pretty good job in  supporting jobs  but it does rely on this magic money  tree i mean when you say 400 billion  you know that is many times the annual  budget of the nhs you know we could have  spent that on improving the nhs and  saving generations of the future so i  think overall  uh in the health department they're  completely ignorant of how business  works and  even what the other effects on health  are of this virus  in the treasury they are giving what  support they can i mean i'd like to say  that  you know on a more positive note and  having um  complained about government i mean we  intend to come out of this crisis  to invest you know i i still plan to  open  a new restaurant on december the 9th so  very shortly we've been working on it  all through lockdown  a new copper club in in cobham we raised  25 million for our restaurants we've  raised 300 million dollars for another  uh vehicle i intend as soon as these  ridiculous measures come to an end  i do intend to invest and start  employing people  and reopen the high street and get  things going again so i'm not and i'm  sure that  the government will try to support that  they just need to focus on the damage  they're doing in the meantime  and and get out of the way you know use  their testing program to protect the  vulnerable  rather than test all these students let  the rest of us get on with it so we can  stop  a a crisis that could turn into a three  four  five year um economic event  now we they need to let us invest so we  can get on with stopping that get on  with the recovery  definitely good to see that uh you're  still planning to invest i'm sure you  have floods of applicants for those jobs  when they are advertised and just  quickly because we are  running out of time um the chief  executive of uk hospitality said  in today's sunday times that if the tier  system lasts till easter  we'll be walking through the smoldering  ruins of an industry that was the third  largest in the uk are you also worried  that this  sector so important to the uk economy  may never actually recover from this  well never's a long time sophie um but i  think  the every day that goes by  uh more owner operator and big  businesses  go out of business thanks to these  measures so  yes i think many people are  many people are closing their doors  every day now um  and just to be clear you know under tier  two let alone tier three  most hospitality businesses are not  viable  and for many people that is their entire  livelihood  so yes if this were to go on in through  to  easter i i think a very huge fraction of  the industry  would close many individual owner  operators many people who work in those  businesses would lose their jobs  and it would take years to recover years  to recover you know we we would  you know we will be trying to recover  and reopen the high street but it will  take years if they carry on with these  measures  um i just think they need to get out  there and see the actual people  and they will see that this is not you  know just voices for the hospitality  industry  making scary statements like they tend  to do with with charts and predictive  models  this is not a model you know this is not  a model that looks to  see into the future like some fortune  teller or some  oracle or the prophets in the bible like  they use their mob  models this is stuff that's actually  happening now people are losing their  jobs  now you know they are going out of  business now they are losing the way to  support their families now  you know it's already happening it's not  something that will happen it is  happening  right now okay um strong words there  thank you  very much for coming on the program and  talking a little bit about the impact on  the hospitality  industry thank you thanks thank you very  much

